BIOMEZONES hooks for files owned by other jobs. Apply with `git apply`.

1. game/scripts/world/island_runtime.gd (ISLANDGEN): one field, so `rt.biome_types` is a real
   property instead of the metadata slot BiomeZones uses today. BiomeZones.install(rt, arr) should
   then assign rt.biome_types, and BiomeZones.biome_types(rt) should return it (both are one-line
   changes in biome_zones.gd, left for the merge so both shapes cannot drift).

--- a/game/scripts/world/island_runtime.gd
+++ b/game/scripts/world/island_runtime.gd
@@ -31,6 +31,9 @@
 var _ridge_dir: Vector2 = Vector2.RIGHT
 var _tile_span: int = 0
 var tile_types: PackedByteArray = PackedByteArray()
+## Biome zone per tile (BiomeZones.ZONE_NAMES index), as painted into the tile map's G channel.
+## BiomeZones.install() writes it; props and scatter read BiomeZones.density(), not this array.
+var biome_types: PackedByteArray = PackedByteArray()
 ## 1 on the river's MUD tiles as built; IslandTerrain.fresh_water_at_world reads it, harvesting never changes it.
 var fresh_water: PackedByteArray = PackedByteArray()
 var _tile_map_image: Image

2. game/scripts/world/island_terrain.gd, the two hard-coded seeds (ISLANDGEN's part of the file;
   the biome part is BIOMEZONES' and is already changed): `var seed := 25 if climate == "temperate" else 17`
   in build() and the same expression inside _build_tile_types' river_center. The biome code reads the
   island id through IslandTerrain._layout_seed() for its moisture field; once ISLANDGEN passes a
   per-island seed (`size_m`, `seed`, shape centre) those expressions should become that seed, and
   _layout_seed can read it instead of World.island_id.

3. game/scripts/world/island_scatter.gd (SCATTER): multiply a family's count by the zone under it,
   e.g. after `var n := ...` from the role table:
     n = int(round(float(n) * BiomeZones.density(BiomeZones.biome_at(rt, wx, wz), group)))
   with group = "tree"/"bush"/"rock"/"plant"; ground cover uses BiomeZones.prop_density(zone, "grass")
   or ("flower"). Per-zone weights need each candidate's (wx, wz) before the count is fixed, so this
   suits the per-instance placement loop better than the per-family total.
